This code uses the `libgpiod` library to interact with the GPIO pins on the Raspberry Pi. It opens the GPIO chip, requests the buzzer and sensor lines, and sets them to output and input modes respectively. It then sets the buzzer to off and the sensor to input.

The `handle_sensor_change` function is called every time the sensor state changes, and it logs the state change to the `obstacle_log.txt` file. If the sensor detects an obstacle, it sets the buzzer to on. If the sensor detects no obstacle, it sets the buzzer to off.

The code also registers signal handlers for `SIGINT` and `SIGTERM` to handle graceful shutdown. When the program receives a `SIGINT` or `SIGTERM` signal, it releases the GPIO lines and closes the chip, and then exits.

Note that this code assumes that the buzzer and sensor are connected to the Raspberry Pi's GPIO pins 18 and 17, respectively. If your hardware is different, you will need to modify the code accordingly.

