This code uses the `pigpio` library to interface with the Raspberry Pi's GPIO and I2C peripherals. It initializes the pigpio library, opens the I2C bus, and sets up a signal handler for SIGINT and SIGTERM to handle graceful shutdown.

The `read_temp()` function reads the temperature register from the LM75B sensor using the I2C bus, converts the data to a signed 9-bit format, and stores it in a circular buffer. The `compute_avg()` function computes the running average of the temperature readings in the circular buffer. The `control_fan()` function controls the cooling fan based on the running average, turning it on when the temperature exceeds 28.0 °C and off when it falls below 26.0 °C.

The main loop reads the temperature, computes the average, and controls the fan every second. When the program receives a SIGINT or SIGTERM signal, it gracefully shuts down by closing the I2C handle and terminating the pigpio library.

