This code uses the `libgpiod` library to interact with the GPIO pins on the Raspberry Pi. It sets up signal handlers for `SIGINT` and `SIGTERM` to handle graceful shutdown, and uses the `gpiod_chip_open_by_name` function to open the GPIO chip. It then uses the `gpiod_chip_get_line` function to get the lines for the DOUT, SCK, and LOCK pins, and sets them up as output lines using the `gpiod_line_request_output` function.

The `tare_sensor` function uses the HX711 bit-bang read protocol to read the weight of the load cell and compute the tare offset. It sets the DOUT and SCK pins to 0, clocks out 24 bits MSB-first, then sends one additional clock pulse to select Channel A / Gain 128. It then averages 10 readings and computes the tare offset as the average of the raw readings.

The `read_weight` function uses the HX711 bit-bang read protocol to read the weight of the load cell and compute the weight in grams. It sets the DOUT and SCK pins to 0, clocks out 24 bits MSB-first, then sends one additional clock pulse to select Channel A / Gain 128. It then averages 5 readings and computes the weight as (average_raw - tare_offset) / scale.

The `lock_solenoid` function sets the LOCK pin to 1 for 200 ms to trigger the solenoid lock.

The main loop reads the weight every 500 ms and logs the weight to the console. If the weight exceeds the threshold, it triggers the solenoid lock.

The code also releases all GPIO lines and chips, closes the syslog, and ensures the solenoid is set LOW on every exit path. It accepts optional command-line arguments for the threshold, scale, and duration, and polls the weight every 500 ms in the main loop.

